WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
In chapter one, separase cut the cohesin links, and the sisters began to part.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.700
But what carries them apart?

3
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.260
Two things move at once.

4
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:14.593
Watch two distances.

5
00:00:16.113 --> 00:00:18.913
In anaphase A, each sister moves toward its pole.

6
00:00:20.193 --> 00:00:23.153
In anaphase B, the poles themselves move apart.

7
00:00:24.413 --> 00:00:26.793
The first changes chromosome-to-pole distance.

8
00:00:28.073 --> 00:00:30.213
The second changes pole-to-pole distance.

9
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:36.513
In anaphase A, each k-fibre, the bundle from a kinetochore to its pole, gets

10
00:00:36.513 --> 00:00:41.713
shorter. A k-fibre contains many microtubules, not one rope.

11
00:00:42.913 --> 00:00:44.353
But where does it lose its subunits?

12
00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:51.477
In 1988, Gorbsky, Sammak and Borisy made the fibres glow in cultured mammalian

13
00:00:51.477 --> 00:00:56.497
cells. Then they bleached a dark stripe across the anaphase spindle.

14
00:00:57.697 --> 00:01:01.317
They tracked poles, chromosomes, and the stripe in living cells.

15
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.900
If the pole reeled the fibres in, the stripe would ride toward the pole with the

16
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.240
chromosome. So which way does the stripe go?

17
00:01:15.783 --> 00:01:19.383
The stripe did not move detectably relative to the near pole.

18
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:25.203
The chromosome moved toward it, shortening the fibre between kinetochore and stripe.

19
00:01:26.463 --> 00:01:29.583
So these fibres lost subunits mainly at the kinetochore.

20
00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:33.203
A small loss elsewhere was not ruled out.

21
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:38.903
Poleward flux means the microtubule lattice itself moves toward the pole.

22
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:45.243
In fruit-fly embryos and frog egg-extract spindles, flux can contribute much more.

23
00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.487
Anaphase B has its own engines.

24
00:01:49.827 --> 00:01:54.327
In many animal cells, motors slide overlapping midzone fibres apart.

25
00:01:55.787 --> 00:01:59.547
Dynein at the cell cortex, its edge, pulls on astral fibres.

26
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:02.847
Their balance differs among cell types.

27
00:02:03.817 --> 00:02:06.397
Now read a movie, with example numbers.

28
00:02:07.837 --> 00:02:11.477
Each kinetochore closes on its pole at 1 µm a minute.

29
00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:15.397
The poles separate at 1 µm a minute.

30
00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:19.217
These are illustrative, not a universal speed.

31
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.440
So how fast do the sisters separate?

32
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.740
Each closes on its own pole while the poles move apart.

33
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.240
2 × 1, plus 1: 3 µm a minute.

34
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.220
In this symmetric movie, sister separation is twice A plus B.

35
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:43.820
Hide the poles. A movie tracking only chromosomes sees that three, but cannot tell

36
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.160
how much came from the poles.

37
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.380
Without that landmark, A and B are indistinguishable.

38
00:02:50.733 --> 00:02:55.693
The two motions add: chromosomes travel along shortening fibres while the poles

39
00:02:55.693 --> 00:02:59.673
spread apart. Measure each against its own landmark.

40
00:03:00.993 --> 00:03:03.573
Those landmarks keep the rates from being confused.

41
00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:09.173
Sisters part by two motions: chromosomes to poles, poles apart.
